The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ret > 0) {
    if (ret != rq->bio.bi_iter.bi_size) {
        bio_advance(&rq->bio, ret);
        zero_fill_bio(&rq->bio);
    }
    ret = 0;
}
```
Reason:  
When a read request goes beyond the end of the filesystem, `vfs_iter_read` returns 0. The condition `if (ret > 0)` skips zero‑filling entirely for `ret == 0`, leaving the bio unfilled/with uninitialized data. Additionally, after setting `ret = 0`, the subsequent error‑handling logic (`ret < 0` checks) fails to propagate the incomplete‑read condition (e.g., for the `bi_end_io` path).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (ret >= 0 && ret != rq->bio.bi_iter.bi_size) {
    bio_advance(&rq->bio, ret);
    zero_fill_bio(&rq->bio);
}
```
```c
if (!rq->bio.bi_end_io) {
    bio_for_each_folio_all(fi, &rq->bio) {
        DBG_BUGON(folio_test_uptodate(fi.folio));
        erofs_onlinefolio_end(fi.folio, ret < 0, false);
    }
} else if (ret < 0 && !rq->bio.bi_status) {
    rq->bio.bi_status = errno_to_blk_status(ret);
}
```
Reason:  
Changing the condition to `ret >= 0` ensures that zero‑filling is performed even when `ret == 0` (end‑of‑file), correctly zeroing out the remaining part of the bio. The restructured error handling passes `ret < 0` (a boolean) to `erofs_onlinefolio_end` when `bi_end_io` is not set, and sets `bi_status` only on actual errors when `bi_end_io` is set. This properly propagates the failure/incomplete status for both paths.

